mem.writeStr((strlit_0_I7463840659243061209_aow7lb5cq1 + 12), "\n/* ---- aowli JIT string marshaling ---------------------------------------- */\n/* The WHOLE 16-byte struct is the inline buffer: byte 0 is the length and bytes\n   1.. are the data, so `more_0` holds CHARACTERS for a medium string and is not\n   a discriminator. A length byte above the inline maximum cannot be an inline\n   length, so that is the \"payload lives in more_0\" marker (the compiler emits\n   254 for a literal and 255 for a heap string). Reading `more_0` as a pointer\n   whenever it was non-zero is what segfaulted on the first COMPUTED 8-byte\n   string: \"<medium>\" stores '>' (0x3e) there.\n\n   AND THE MAXIMUM IS 14, NOT 15, however much room the struct looks like it\n   has. `stringimpl.nim` sets `PayloadSize = (sizeof(uint) - 1) + sizeof(ptr) -\n   1` = 14 and `len()` reads `more.fullLen` for ANY length byte ABOVE that - so\n   a 15-byte string written inline here is read back by the runtime as a heap\n   string whose `more` pointer is its own last eight characters. It dereferences\n   them. Measured: `aoughwl.settings` crossing `stateKey(s: Setting)`, whose\n   `key` is \"airAcceleration\" - fifteen letters - segfaulted inside the shimmed\n   proc on the first call. Nothing of 0..14 or of 16-and-up was ever wrong,\n   which is why this survived: it is one length. */\n#define AOWLI_JIT_INLINE_MAX 14\n\nstatic long long aowli_jit_slen(");

